   "stem": {
      "text": "The graph of the function f shown is defined for \\( 0 \\le x \\le 8 \\), except where the graph shows no filled point. Find \\( \\lim_{x \\to 5} f(x) \\). If the limit does not exist, say why.",
      "command_verb": "find"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "statement",
      "label": "The limit does not exist, because f(x) approaches different values from the left and from the right of x = 5."
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "As x approaches 5 from the left, the graph approaches the open circle at (5, 1), so the left hand limit is 1.",
         "rule_named": "one sided limit read from a graph"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "As x approaches 5 from the right, the graph approaches the open circle at (5, -3), so the right hand limit is -3.",
         "rule_named": "one sided limit read from a graph"
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The one sided limits differ, so \\( \\lim_{x \\to 5} f(x) \\) does not exist, whatever the value of f at 5.",
         "rule_named": "two sided limit exists only when the one sided limits agree"
      }
